-- title:
--   Theorem 1.1 (corrected: Corollary 3.5 integrand $\varphi(s-1/2)$): for $n\ge3$ rays, $2\sqrt{U(0,0,0)}$ is the best constant in $\mathbb E D_\tau\le C\sqrt{\mathbb E\tau}$
-- statement:
--   Let $n\ge3$ and let $S$ be the spider process on $n$ rays, built on a probability space carrying a Brownian motion $B$ and independent uniform complex signs $\theta$. For a stopping time $\tau$ of the filtration generated by $S$, let $D_\tau$ be the diameter, in the British rail metric, of the trajectory $\{S_s: s\le\tau\}$: the sum of its two longest ribs on distinct rays. Then the best constant $C_n$ in the inequality
--   $$\mathbb E D_\tau\le C_n\sqrt{\mathbb E\tau}\qquad\text{for all stopping times } \tau \text{ of } S \text{ with } \mathbb E\tau<\infty \tag{1.6}$$
--   is
--   $$C_n=2\sqrt{U(0,0,0)},\qquad U(0,0,0)=\int_{-1/2}^{0}U(\tfrac12,\tfrac12,s)\,\frac{2n(n-1)}{(n-1-2s)^2}\,ds,$$
--   where $U(\tfrac12,\tfrac12,\cdot)$ is the explicit function of Section 3 ((3.8) and Corollary 3.5). That is, (1.6) holds with $C_n=2\sqrt{U(0,0,0)}$, and it fails for every smaller constant.
--
--   For $n=3$, $C_3\approx1.7776$; for $n=1,2$ the corresponding constants are $\sqrt2$ and $\sqrt3$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Expectations are lower Lebesgue integrals in $[0,\infty]$ and (1.6) reads $\int D_\tau\,dP\le C\sqrt{\mathbb E\tau}$ (with the right side $0$ when $C<0$); "best constant" is the least element of the set of real constants for which (1.6) holds.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: Corollary 3.5 integrand (n − 1)(s − x)/((n − 1)s − φ(y − 1/2)) (and ((n − 1)s − x)/(…) for x ≤ 0) → denominator (n − 1)s − φ(s − 1/2); reason: the printed version contradicts (3.9) of Lemma 3.4, which it integrates, and continuity of U across y = 1/2: at n = 3, (x, z) = (−0.05, −0.1), (3.12) tends to 0.94325 as y ↑ 1/2 while the printed formula gives 0.97892 at y = 1/2 (corrected: 0.94325); at n = 2 the printed chain gives U(0,0,0) = 0.7616 instead of the known 3/4
--   - reading: "U(0, 0, 0) is defined in Corollary 3.6" → U(0,0,0) is (3.13) at the origin with U(1/2, 1/2, s) the explicit value from (3.8) and Corollary 3.5, and φ^{−1} the two-branch f; reason: p.18: "(3.13) can be applied for x = y = z = 0, resulting in quite an involved, yet nonetheless explicit expression"
--   - encoding: E D_τ ≤ C_n √(E τ); best constant → ∫⁻ D_τ dP ≤ ofReal(C · √((∫⁻ τ dP).toReal)) for all τ with ∫⁻ τ dP < ∞; IsLeast over C ∈ ℝ; reason: expectations of nonnegative variables in [0, ∞]; E τ is finite, so the real square root is exact
--   - encoding: stopping time of S → τ : Ω → [0, ∞] with {τ ≤ t} ∈ σ(S_s : s ≤ t) for every t; D_τ is the diameter of {S_s : s ≤ τ} (the whole path where τ = ∞, a null set); reason: the paper's filtration generated by S, with Mathlib's {τ ≤ t} convention
--   - encoding: |T_t ∩ R_k|, the length of the rib → Lebesgue measure of {r ≥ 0 : r e^{2πi(k+1)/n} ∈ T_t}; reason: the length of the subset of the ray, transported by the isometry r ↦ r e^{2πi(k+1)/n}
--   - encoding: B is a Brownian motion (a.s. continuous paths, B_0 = 0 a.s.) → every path of B is continuous and starts at 0, and every B_t is measurable; reason: the paper's Brownian motion (Revuz–Yor) is the continuous version; every Brownian motion has such a version
--   - encoding: rays R_k, k = 1, …, n, and complex signs uniform on {e^{2πik/n}} → rays indexed from 0 (Fin n, resp. ℕ); index k is the paper's ray k + 1; the sign θ_m is recorded by its ray index; reason: relabelling; same instances
--   - encoding: the countable set of excursions "can be ordered by the set of natural numbers" (order unspecified) → excursion number = least k such that the k-th enumerated nonnegative rational lies in the excursion; reason: one concrete injective, path-measurable numbering; the law of the spider does not depend on the numbering
--   - encoding: D_t = max over pairs j ≠ k of the sum of two ribs → ⨆ over j ≠ k in [0, ∞]; reason: the index set is finite and nonempty for n ≥ 3, so the supremum is the maximum
-- source:
--   Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, On the Diameter of the Stopped Spider Process, arXiv:2105.15202v2, Theorem 1.1, p.3; inequality (1.6), p.3; stopping times of S, p.2; U(0,0,0) from (3.13), p.18 with Corollary 3.5, p.17 (corrected)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Noise
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Spider
import Definitions.Def_SpiderDiameter_Candidate

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal ENNReal

namespace SpiderDiameter

/-- **Theorem 1.1** (Bednarz–Ernst–Osękowski, *On the Diameter of the Stopped Spider Process*,
arXiv:2105.15202v2, Theorem 1.1, p.3): "For n ≥ 3, the best constant in (1.6) is given by
C_n = 2√U(0, 0, 0), where U(0, 0, 0) is defined in Corollary 3.6 below." Inequality (1.6), p.3,
is `E D_τ ≤ C_n √(E τ)` for every integrable stopping time `τ` of the spider process `S`
(p.2).

For every `n ≥ 3` and every probability space carrying the Brownian motion `B` and the
independent uniform ray indices `θ`, the number `2 √(U(0,0,0))` is the least real `C` such that
`E D_τ ≤ C √(E τ)` for every stopping time `τ` of the filtration generated by `S` with
`E τ < ∞`. Here `U(0,0,0) = candidate n 0 0 0` is (3.13) at the origin,
`∫_{−1/2}^{0} U(1/2, 1/2, s) · 2n(n−1)/(n−1−2s)² ds`.

**Formalization Note.** Expectations are lower Lebesgue integrals in `[0, ∞]`; `D_τ` is the
diameter (1.5) of the trajectory up to `τ`. The constant uses Corollary 3.5 with the corrected
integrand `φ(s − 1/2)` (printed `φ(y − 1/2)`), see `integrand35`. Rays are indexed by `Fin n`,
excursions are numbered through an enumeration of the rationals, and `B` is the continuous
version (see `SpiderNoise`, `excIndex`). -/
theorem theorem_1_1 (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω] (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (N : SpiderNoise n P) :
    IsLeast {C : ℝ | ∀ τ : Ω → ℝ≥0∞, IsSpiderStoppingTime N τ → ∫⁻ ω, τ ω ∂P < ⊤ →
        ∫⁻ ω, diam N (τ ω) ω ∂P ≤ ENNReal.ofReal (C * Real.sqrt (∫⁻ ω, τ ω ∂P).toReal)}
      (2 * Real.sqrt (candidate n 0 0 0)) := by sorry

end SpiderDiameter
